<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gers</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gers is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, named after the river Gers, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>. A rural area of hills, it covers the historic Armagnac district, known for its brandy, and in antiquity was home to the Ausci and Elusates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gers.jpg" alt="Gers" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Auch)_Fa%C3%A7ade_ouest_de_la_pr%C3%A9fecture_du_Gers.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Gers is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It is bordered by the departments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hautes-pyrenees">Hautes-Pyrénées</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Pyrénées-Atlantiques</a> to the south, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haute-garonne">Haute-Garonne</a> and Tarn-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> to the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne">Lot-et-Garonne</a> to the north and Landes to the west. Its inhabitants are called Gersois and Gersoises, after the Gers River. The landscape is one of sleepy bastide villages and rolling hills, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> visible to the south. Annual rainfall varies from more than 900 mm in the south-west to less than 700 mm in the north-east, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a>, Condom and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lectoure">Lectoure</a>. Winters are mild and summers hot and dry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Gers is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, on 4 March 1790. It was formed from parts of the former provinces of Guyenne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>. In 1808 it lost Lavit, on its north-eastern side, to the newly created department of Tarn-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The culture is largely agricultural, with weight placed on local food and drink: Armagnac brandy, Côtes de Gascogne wine, Floc de Gascogne, foie gras and wild mushrooms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">Maize</a>, colza, sunflowers and grain are the main crops. Gascon, a dialect of Occitan, is spoken only by a minority. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> is among the most thinly peopled and most rural areas in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>, with about 31 people per square kilometre and a population of 192,645 in 2023.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-dumas">Alexandre Dumas</a>, père, made the Gersois d'Artagnan the fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musketeer</a> of The Three Musketeers, and a museum to d'Artagnan stands in the village of Lupiac. A horse race at the Auteuil Hippodrome is named after André Boingnères, a local race-horse owner and mayor of Termes-d'Armagnac from 1951 to 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ausci lived in the hills of central Gascony, in the modern <b>Gers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ausci">Ausci</a></li><li><blockquote>The Elusates lived in the north-west of the modern <b>Gers</b>, in the Armagnac hills.</blockquote><a href="/state/elusates">Elusates</a></li></ul></section>
